<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no" ?>
<FinalDraft DocumentType="Script" Template="No" Version="1">

  <Content>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ACT I</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. FATAK'S HOUSE - KITCHEN - AFTERNOON</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The rich, spiced aroma of Chicken Biryani fills the air. Sunlight streams through the open window, catching the dust motes dancing above a steaming pot.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>MOTHER (40s, tired but loving) stirs the pot with a large wooden spoon.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FATAK (14, energetic, entirely focused on his stomach) bounces on his heels behind her, holding a large empty plate.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Is it ready? The aroma is driving me mad!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MOTHER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Patience, Fatak. The rice needs to breathe.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I cannot breathe without it! You know it's my fuel. Chicken biryani, every single day. That's the rule of life.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MOTHER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(Sighs, smiling)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>A very expensive rule. Here.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She heaps a massive mound of Biryani onto his plate. Two large chicken legs sit atop the mountain of fragrant rice.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. DINING ROOM - MOMENTS LATER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak sits alone at the table. He attacks the food with zero eating discipline, rapidly shoveling spoonfuls into his mouth.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Beside him sits a steaming cup of CHAI.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Parenthetical">
      <Text>(To himself)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Nothing else matters. Just this.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He takes a long, loud sip of tea, leaning back in absolute gluttonous satisfaction.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ACT II</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. FATAK'S STREET - EVENING</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The sun is setting, casting long, golden shadows across the pavement. The evening breeze rustles the leaves of a large Banyan tree at the end of the street, bringing a sudden chill.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak and his friend RAJU (14) are playing catch with a worn-out tennis ball.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAJU</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Think fast!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Raju hurls the ball with sudden urgency. It sails high over Fatak's head and drops over the crumbling brick wall of the neighboring, abandoned compound.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Ah, Raju! Now I have to go into the overgrown lot.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>RAJU</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Hurry! It's getting dark.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>EXT. NEIGHBORING COMPOUND - CONTINUOUS</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak climbs over the low wall and drops into the thick, untamed weeds. The sounds of the busy street immediately fade, replaced by the chirping of crickets and the quiet rustle of nature.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He searches for the ball in the fading twilight.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Suddenly, he freezes.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Through the tangled branches of a bougainvillea bush, he sees an OLD MAN sitting on a cracked stone bench.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Old Man is frail, wearing tattered, threadbare clothes. In his trembling hands is a small, chipped bowl containing a mere handful of plain, watery white rice.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak hides behind the bush, holding his breath.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The Old Man doesn't complain. He eats the meager portion of rice with intense care, picking up every single grain as if it were precious. He pauses, looking up at the evening sky, taking a slow, deep breath, finding a quiet dignity in the moment despite his profound lack of resources.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak looks down at his own stomach, then back at the Old Man. The memory of his careless, daily mountain of Biryani flashes vividly in his mind. The contrast hits him like a physical blow.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The ball rests at Fatak's feet. He slowly picks it up, the joy of the game completely gone. A sharp realization of the world's harsh reality takes its place.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>ACT III</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Scene Heading">
      <Text>INT. DINING ROOM - THE NEXT AFTERNOON</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>The same afternoon sunlight. The same steaming cup of tea.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mother walks in and places the usual, massive mountain of Chicken Biryani in front of Fatak.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MOTHER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Eat up quickly. You were so quiet after your game yesterday.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>She walks back to the kitchen.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak stares at the plate. The aroma is there, but his appetite has vanished. He picks up a spoon and simply moves the rice around.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He closes his eyes and sees the Old Man carefully, gratefully eating his plain rice.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak acts with sudden decisiveness. He pushes the heavy plate away and grabs a smaller, empty bowl from the center of the table.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>With deliberate care, he transfers exactly HALF of the Biryani into the small bowl, leaving the rest untouched on the platter.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mother walks back in, wiping her hands on a towel. She stops dead in her tracks, shocked.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MOTHER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Fatak? Are you sick? You haven't devoured it yet. And why is it separated?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak looks up. His eyes are clear, filled with a sudden maturity and purpose.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I am not sick, Ma. I am just... awake.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MOTHER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Awake to what? It's your favorite. Chicken Biryani.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It is. And I am grateful for it. But I don't need this much. Look at this mountain. It's too much for one person while others have absolutely nothing.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MOTHER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>What are you talking about?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yesterday, I saw an old man making do with a handful of plain rice. He ate it with such care, just sitting quietly under the trees. It made me realize how much I take for granted. My greed was blinding me.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak looks down at the smaller, modest portion in his bowl.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>From today, I will practice eating discipline. I will eat only half of what I usually demand.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>MOTHER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>But you are a growing boy!</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I will grow better on less. This frugality... it's a promise to myself. If I embrace a minimalist way, this same quantity of food will last us twice as long. And maybe, in some small way, choosing not to consume everything will mean there are more resources left in the world for someone like that old man.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak reaches into his pocket and pulls out a small notebook and a pencil.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>FATAK (CONT'D)</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I'm going to write this promise down. Words make things real. They help us commit, and maybe if others read it, they'll understand too. It's how we can work together to change things.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>He carefully writes his vow on the blank page, acting immediately on his realization.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Mother watches him, her worry melting into profound respect. She steps forward and places a gentle hand on his shoulder.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>Fatak takes a small bite from his halved portion. He chews slowly, consciously appreciating the life-giving nourishment of every single grain.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Action">
      <Text>FADE OUT.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
  </Content>
</FinalDraft>
